{"availability":"available","full":"ruby-next/ruby-next","name":"ruby-next","description":"Ruby Next makes modern Ruby code run in older versions and alternative implementations","category":"Developer tools","language":"Ruby","stars":800,"forks":43,"created":"2019-10-25T14:05:53Z","updated":"2026-07-25T05:06:19Z","license":"MIT","branch":"master","homepage":"https://ruby-next.github.io","topics":["hacktoberfest","ruby","transpiler"],"demo":"https://ruby-next.github.io/","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T21:19:37.213Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-03T21:19:37.213Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-03T21:19:37.213Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-03T21:19:37.213Z","stars":800,"forks":43}],"technologies":[],"lastDemoCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T09:39:40.683Z","overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Ruby Next is a transpiler and a collection of polyfills for supporting the latest and upcoming Ruby features (APIs and syntax) in older versions and alternative implementations. For example, you can use pattern matching and Kernel#then in Ruby 2.5 or mruby.","Ruby Next also aims to help the community to assess new, experimental, MRI features by making it easier to play with them. That's why Ruby Next implements the master features as fast as possible.","Ruby Next is built by Evil Martians, an American design and engineering consultancy for developer tools, AI, and cybersecurity startups."],"features":["Ruby gems maintainers who want to write code using the latest Ruby version but still support older ones.","Application developers who want to give new features a try without waiting for the final release (or, more often, for the first patch).","Users of non-MRI implementations such as mruby, JRuby, TruffleRuby, Natalie, Opal, RubyMotion, Artichoke, Prism.","Ruby syntax enthusiasts who want to experiment with custom syntax extensions 👩‍🔬👨‍🔬."],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/ruby-next/ruby-next/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"d8be7c5634f1ed9996fc2db0